## Hub safety (every agent)

You are running inside a live Shelbi hub: the daemon that hosts your own
session also hosts every other agent's session and the user's sidebar. A
process-level mistake here takes down everyone, not just you.

- Never run `shelbi daemon stop`, `shelbi daemon restart`, `shelbi reload`,
  `shelbi quit`, `shelbi session kill`, or `shelbi workspace stop` from a
  worker pane. Those are the user's and the orchestrator's commands.
- Never kill shelbi or agent processes by name pattern (`pkill`, `killall`,
  `pgrep | xargs kill`). Every session and agent on the hub runs the same
  binaries, so a pattern cannot tell yours apart from everyone else's. Kill
  only a pid you started yourself.
- When you must exercise daemon or session start/stop code, do it inside an
  isolated root: a fresh temp dir in `SHELBI_ROOT` (or `SHELBI_HOME`) AND
  `env -u SHELBI_HUB_SOCK -u SHELBI_CONTROL_SOCK`, with its own pid file and
  sockets. Confirm the pid you are about to signal came from that sandbox's
  pid file, never from `~/.shelbi/shelbi.pid`.
- Tests must not touch the real `~/.shelbi`, the real hub socket, or the real
  daemon lock. Every test that spawns or stops a daemon sets its own root.

## Jobs

Run a long command (a build, a test suite, a migration) as a Shelbi job
instead of blocking on it; run quick ones directly:

- `shelbi job run --label "<label>" --estimate <duration> -- <command>`
  prints a job id and returns at once. Keep working on something that doesn't
  need the result, or end your turn.
- When the job finishes, its result (status, exit code, last output line)
  arrives in this session as a prompt. So does a note if it runs past its
  estimate.
- `shelbi job log <id> -n 50` prints the end of its output (no `-n` for all
  of it, `-f` to follow a running job to its end).
- `shelbi job list` shows running jobs; `--all` adds finished ones.
  `shelbi job estimate <id> <duration>` revises an estimate, `shelbi job
  cancel <id>` stops a job.
- If `shelbi` isn't on your PATH (a remote machine without it), run the
  command directly.

## Wiki

The project's wiki holds its plans, designs, decisions and other long-form
context, shared by everyone on the project. Before starting work, read the plan
your task names, or look for one with `shelbi wiki search "<topic>"`. Write any
plan, design doc or decision record there (`shelbi wiki new Plans/<name>.md`),
never as a file in the repo. Read and write it only through `shelbi wiki`; the
hub commits and syncs every change. The `shelbi-wiki` skill has the commands and
the rules.
